      "new_sha256": "2566a25dca02970a7b197b81e8b33097f814983d9d7982a0c8c0b0b5e507c16f",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-478",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we select the identity that contains a $\\tan^2{x}$ term. So we can use the identity \n\n$$\n1+\\tan^2x =\\sec^2x.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above identity gives\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x =\\sec^2 x -1.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int \\tan^2 x \\, \\textrm{d}x&=\\int \\left(\\sec^2 x -1\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n\t\t\t\t&=\\tan x -x+C.\t\n\t}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007646/example-478.json",
          "file_sha256": "5aa058c8db4b888fa28de731cf2c707eac712f54c3919fed45d4e65cb913a59a"
        }
      ],
