    {
      "question": "e-480",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "303064d8d2786c2e0c053d40ebe36a599494b8bc747d2b8eb34a7b5973f9b14d",
      "new_sha256": "0c25cead7964ac9afefab5fbe102fb06e5ff3815a1380fa7b337f8c768599e42",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-480",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "From the identity  $1+\\tan^2 \\theta =\\sec^2 \\theta$ with $\\theta = 3x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t1+\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x)\\\n\t\t\\tan^2 (3x) &=\\sec^2 (3x) -1.\n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\int 2\\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x&= 2\\int \\tan^2 (3x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n                                                                &= 2\\int \\left(\\sec^2 (3x) -1\\right ) \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n                                                                 &= 2\\left(\\int \\sec^2 (3x)\\, \\textrm{d}x -\\int1 \\, \\textrm{d}x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t                                &= 2\\left( \\dfrac{1}{3}\\tan (3x) -x\\right)+C\\\n                                                                 &= \t\\dfrac{2}{3}\\tan (3x) -2x+C.\n\t}\n$$",
